      "claim": "A rational consumer aims to maximize total net benefit, defined as total benefit minus total cost.",
      "evidence": "Standard microeconomic assumption of utility maximization.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The optimal quantity of consumption is achieved where the marginal benefit of the last unit consumed equals the marginal cost of that unit.",
      "evidence": "First-order condition for maximization: if MB > MC, consuming more increases net benefit; if MB < MC, consuming less increases net benefit. Thus, the maximum occurs where MB = MC.",
      "weight": 0.4,
      "depends_on": [
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The consumer stops eating when the marginal benefit of the next cookie equals the marginal cost of that next cookie.",
      "evidence": "Application of the optimization rule to the discrete context of eating cookies; consumption ceases when the condition for further net benefit gain (MB > MC) no longer holds.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [
        2
      ]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "B",
  "confidence": 1.0,
  "considered": [
    {
      "answer": "A",
      "reason_rejected": "Total benefit equals total cost implies zero net benefit (break-even), which is not the condition for maximizing net benefit. Maximization occurs where the *change* in benefit equals the *change* in cost (marginal analysis)."
    },
    {
      "answer": "C",
      "reason_rejected": "Maximizing marginal cost is not a rational stopping rule; rational consumers compare benefits to costs, not just maximize costs."
    },
    {
      "answer": "D",
      "reason_rejected": "Minimizing marginal benefit (often to zero) ignores the cost side of the equation. The stopping condition requires balancing benefit against cost, not just minimizing benefit."
    }
  ]
